#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "df884ed9-c75e-5a38-abe2-425e01ee4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-432" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "317454f0-3a04-5c1c-a451-2b8b2ec850ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "189d4a9a-fb2c-57b2-b9f6-9bec14fa1062" :question/math-academy-id "e-434" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8afb12-c143-5d6c-9273-ac8abd85f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} {:answer/id #uuid "65edb11e-1e0e-5d4b-bc4d-5dbf5cafa493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306161} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2c7d95-276c-51d5-8e6e-1785b9a65df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306162} {:answer/id #uuid "d68b4eca-f9a8-5f56-a553-5d20378e9e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306163} {:answer/id #uuid "213a9033-3c23-597e-a74b-6bf809abfba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c8afb12-c143-5d6c-9273-ac8abd85f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306160} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cde449-95b7-5409-84ce-9958ec24578e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "042f12f3-e4d4-5dee-9b70-77f357143515" :question/math-academy-id "q-117680" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f1af6f-a432-58bb-a999-4cd67fd6fa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296670} {:answer/id #uuid "f95ca29d-d08a-5b85-9f41-f3a7fb4bdd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296671} {:answer/id #uuid "cc84a1b4-4b51-505e-a8bc-365bdc846107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296672} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f6f531-5914-5ed2-bc2f-e3069da9a038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296673} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf8bd7-143b-5651-84fb-374769cf2501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f6f531-5914-5ed2-bc2f-e3069da9a038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296673} :answer-field/id #uuid "97582334-4c92-5acc-b560-b65db03a5215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3e6f460-32b8-5013-a35b-ba52c5b54bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-18004" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecbd94e8-0162-5a46-afb8-a1f117644bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306167} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5c4ebe-ba72-5b6f-9790-2efac1eb642a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306168} {:answer/id #uuid "62743952-0366-51bc-aeca-cea3b44cf55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306169} {:answer/id #uuid "7d112a57-0263-575c-ad33-496e425ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306170} {:answer/id #uuid "baba40d7-0ad2-5dde-9ccf-7de61d5c9aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d112a57-0263-575c-ad33-496e425ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306170} :answer-field/id #uuid "721224c0-794a-527e-b70b-41b87069c5fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f11a9469-77dd-5592-a5e2-e400aca2dd52" :question/math-academy-id "q-29739" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd73cb7b-9901-5d6e-bba2-9419023d3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306174} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7443a1-00ce-54d3-9c5c-554d130cc1ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7443a1-00ce-54d3-9c5c-554d130cc1ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306175} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ea03e0-d23c-564a-ad66-b50e33e59bf0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa1ed858-bd59-5697-b4fd-680cbb34c192" :question/math-academy-id "q-316122" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/64ce1ae5f198ce41e55414d853b1bd99e73d747cddb80412b30246bfdb13a907.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a97442ad-2e34-5024-b4fb-63ff1e470729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a97442ad-2e34-5024-b4fb-63ff1e470729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306178} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad7120e-6272-51a0-92d0-34cc606a28d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7b9963d-ecf3-5165-a89b-7d1a2c7f953c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338319" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/82096496503c058ae31473029f7844ddff99fad024dd786c7bad88985279a9af.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-1,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,6+7.5 \\\\  & =\\,13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132afdfc-3713-5399-8f49-101b47045d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306181} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ccd115-b96f-51d6-8ec8-58d62e231313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ccd115-b96f-51d6-8ec8-58d62e231313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306182} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ec4486-a34c-5ab2-abb2-f87ce2c7f28c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dd9a4c2-9470-5512-b36a-e625f617bf1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338505" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a1a419a984000b3b8559a19809416bcccc9eb8341985994c621c3a48d15109da.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{9}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5d5852-5bb2-560b-b2be-e53077c19cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi}{4}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306185} {:answer/id #uuid "9467e07b-6c43-5850-88f3-9487bfed200d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi}{4}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9467e07b-6c43-5850-88f3-9487bfed200d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi}{4}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306186} :answer-field/id #uuid "5093a3e2-4293-5880-96b6-2b9abe4fc11e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7dbda93-ab32-56ef-a596-f9f3d692e6ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-339677" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/353412402972b390de950f56b3273d8dbecf60d4d07c7c27e9281c28145a86d2.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,4],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [4,7].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a rectangle and a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -6.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,7].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -6-2+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{13\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf407f2-5989-55ec-8135-061fe831e33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289659} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6547f3-a2fc-5780-9052-18f283aedd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf407f2-5989-55ec-8135-061fe831e33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289659} :answer-field/id #uuid "97084882-fc93-5592-8a8d-200228f92323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4321dc5-5a1f-5eae-acf4-e27f4a92f7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-341981" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd7e933-fcb7-5b18-952b-55e57545ab9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dd7e933-fcb7-5b18-952b-55e57545ab9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306190} :answer-field/id #uuid "8166babf-0c39-5911-8ec1-c9b631a872b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3c1829a-8807-56ab-95ed-938993da9f28" :question/math-academy-id "q-342004" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3))]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-(-\\frac{9}{2}-12)]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{7}{2}-(-\\frac{33}{2})]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8038c8-1046-58e8-84f0-5162159f0c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296697} {:answer/id #uuid "48776417-a438-5f07-b0e6-d31ef8f8314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296698} {:answer/id #uuid "891c1d62-d5a1-5127-b5aa-b14807a51b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296699} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb9a25-3870-5d8e-9e54-d2c4d0f5d3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296700} {:answer/id #uuid "102e1f8a-f770-53ca-91bf-9d06ba760e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48776417-a438-5f07-b0e6-d31ef8f8314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a261122-1a15-5bfa-8e2c-cf52c60b75a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97f5c0c0-1b45-5f58-be76-fdc17942bba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353387" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff462772-7b83-513f-ad9d-8e4ef5d0f5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306193} {:answer/id #uuid "edf633c4-274b-570c-a0d0-b65c6697f23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306194} {:answer/id #uuid "8115f8cc-a039-5bf1-84d4-8bb1b1d08234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306195} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1aa191-05da-5e5a-8720-ea487550217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306196} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e2c2f2-e28a-5f34-bf45-10f12eab567f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf633c4-274b-570c-a0d0-b65c6697f23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306194} :answer-field/id #uuid "da1d6452-abf2-58d9-9d50-98c6dad60827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10eefb23-e667-5ee1-a761-4753001a7b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49169" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-2,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2).$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$  The corresponding region is the trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2+4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\pi +\\pi -12=2\\pi -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} {:answer/id #uuid "27e19e5e-9ae9-55e3-a6b1-2d3041c09690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076889} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa18fed-eec9-5887-a749-9f71fb14a6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076890} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8ad594-e424-5b7f-9948-5e8da6618757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076891} {:answer/id #uuid "33d74abb-4ac1-5932-857d-5df1fe3e33ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7304737-cba6-5fbc-b8be-0f1ac860d786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076888} :answer-field/id #uuid "6463ff6a-e667-5122-8f6a-66c6d265d077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b82eafa4-95ed-5313-a838-9f9ba69b6d8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49176" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/83b23d8138091a931d14854b197752d25d8149ee8914a70b8db73b6c2369c888.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17280961-b8ed-5aa6-81f7-d8b50b1a9829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306200} {:answer/id #uuid "5c96d804-f537-52e4-b34d-fd4d435b98cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306201} {:answer/id #uuid "80b4f644-df23-51c0-8831-2901cfcbe042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306202} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc19608-29e4-5590-8c3a-58a595a50ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306203} {:answer/id #uuid "7e76dd41-e945-5009-a407-63b8f134dd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c96d804-f537-52e4-b34d-fd4d435b98cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306201} :answer-field/id #uuid "f394f966-6c73-55ab-af28-cd289c2d467d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef200c23-7d18-56c9-a45a-07e8091b06fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-49215" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in (-2,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,3].$  So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe third integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid. Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+4)(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =9-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcb1520b-235b-5cea-bacf-623ce39e2497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077044} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b6c71d-8121-5aee-b9b2-bd10a0f6c04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077045} {:answer/id #uuid "12060711-88b3-5b49-854b-07986d37ab6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077046} {:answer/id #uuid "4be47cff-d863-5011-b6f2-6fdb9673542c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077047} {:answer/id #uuid "7af1221c-ba06-5272-96e1-e9e202d9256d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af1221c-ba06-5272-96e1-e9e202d9256d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077048} :answer-field/id #uuid "805565f6-24e6-51c1-97fb-51e6e8617a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7ff75a6-3ae3-540e-8971-b7a98178dcaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-5046" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}4\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-3}^{0}+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =[4(0)-4(-3)]+[-\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2}+4(4)+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}-4(0)] \\\\  & =[0+12]+[-8+16+0-0] \\\\  & =12+8 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65ce43a5-9fe5-5e24-aa7e-54696134f42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306207} {:answer/id #uuid "21d0f3fe-8b92-5098-91da-33a897685f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306208} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb579d9-67a4-53cc-865b-a8c0296d2196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306209} {:answer/id #uuid "23adcbc2-3300-5708-b2e5-a73aa81573bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306210} {:answer/id #uuid "aecb74e4-a6f6-570f-8b69-14c38f61a0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb579d9-67a4-53cc-865b-a8c0296d2196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306209} :answer-field/id #uuid "952dc6b3-1e4e-5f19-8a10-ed619c78c640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b306839f-7159-5629-ab24-554eb2b191f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-5047" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 3, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x){|}_{-5}^{1}+3x{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}+2(1)-\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}-2(-5)]+[3(3)-3(1)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+2-\\frac{25}{2}+10]+[9-3] \\\\  & =0+6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
